/-!
# Saturated distance-two symplectic configurations

The literal saturated radical acts at both endpoints of one critical pair,
with the original symplectic marking and the complete cores as kernels.
Each full fixed attachment contains the core Frattini subgroup and is fixed
by the common radical modulo that subgroup. The whole core modulo its fixed
attachment contains its natural module as a dual cover, yielding
[Q : U C_Q(L)] ≤ q at the original field degree.

The trivial multiplier of the original Sp₄(F) also identifies L ∩ Q = U.
Thus T₀ = T ∩ L supplies the full-radical factorization and the exact index
[T : T₀ C_Q(L)] = [Q : U C_Q(L)]. In the two parabolic cases, both core
kernels contain the common full-radical derived group, and the opposite
T₀-displacements of the complete fixed attachments lie in the opposite
natural core and their own Frattini subgroup.

The paired fixed-kernel equality of MS93 (3.7)(4) is proved below. The
field-sized critical configuration in the parabolic branches and the final
P₁-module application remain to establish the field restriction.
The core-comparison step of (3.7)(5) is also proved: its equal fixed kernels,
equal displacements and contained Z-layer imply equality of the two full
cores and their central omega layers. The equality of the Z-layers is now
proved in both critical-image branches: the Q₂ count and the dual-cover
fixed-vector argument complete source (5), retaining its three antecedents.
Source (6) now has its initial actual element x in the opposite Z-layer,
outside the endpoint core, with C_Z(x) exactly the midpoint omega-center.
The moving normalizer is selected, and its induced midpoint action now gives
another genuine critical pair. The same selected involutions commute. If
the new image is not already of order q, its literal element-centralizer
has both source indices q. Its whole-core displacement now also has exact
index q modulo the entire attachment, excluding full radical movement of
index q squared. The whole-core centralizer bound and collapse now prove existence of an
actual order-q critical pair. The post-(6) star argument makes the moving
fixed attachment trivial at every endpoint, retaining the full fixed core.
The original critical commutator therefore has order q, and the literal
quotient by the full fixed core preserves the overlap index q. These supply
the whole-core and numerical premises for the point-parabolic endgame;
its remaining geometry and the field-four conclusion are still open.

For an actual order-q critical image, the contained-core branch of (3.7)(3)
now proves Q = U C_Q(L) and both actual overlap indices equal q. The
central omega identities X = Z and Z = U Z_t hold without the additional
core-containment premise, retaining the complete fixed attachment.
For an actual order-q critical image, the full radical now centralizes
that image in the opposite quotient and fixes the complete covered moving
commutator. Both T₀-displacements lie in U, and the critical commutator
lies in the midpoint omega-center, including in the Sylow branch.
In the full Sylow branch, odd coset averaging now proves Z = U Z_t
without an order-q premise. Both critical images have order q or both have
order q squared. The opposite natural cover now gives the exact fixed
centralizer count q in the large-image case. The Sylow center and Frattini
comparison exclude that case, so both Sylow critical images have order q.
Full-radical centrality therefore holds on both sides in every saturated
branch. Symplectic orthogonality and fixed-vector lifting also reverse both
Sylow triples through the complete central cover. Thus all of (3.7)(1)
holds, together with the midpoint containment of the critical commutator.
The whole-core centralizer of any actual critical layer now lies in
U C_Q(L), by the dual-cover refinement obstruction. This proves Z = U Z_t
at every endpoint without an order-q premise. When the opposite core moves
at most q squared cosets on U, its full-cover action is quadratic, and the
reversed triple proves [Z′,Q] ≤ Z_t, the first subcase of (2).
In the large-movement subcase, symplectic orthogonality now bounds the
opposite image of T₀ by q. The critical lower bound identifies the T₀, U
and Z images, all of order q, and gives T₀ = U (T₀ ∩ Q′). Both actual
critical images have order q. The full fixed-core action is now proved
quadratic on the opposite original Z-layer. The actual order-q critical
image also puts both complete cover kernels inside the common commutator,
whose order is q times either kernel order. Their orders therefore agree;
equality of the kernels is not yet asserted. The full fixed-core
displacement intersects that commutator only inside the first fixed kernel.
Their natural displacement sum is isotropic, giving at most q additional
cosets and the full bound |[Z′,D]| ≤ q |U ∩ C(L)|. The opposite full fixed-core actor image now has order at most 2q,
using the original central cover and its invariant quadratic refinement.
The component-product comparison bounds the full radical image by 2q³;
exact saturation and q > 2 then force order q³ and exclude the full Sylow
image. The whole radical is now identified with a point radical on the
original ambient cover, retaining its literal quotient marking and full
core kernel. The point-cover squeeze also gives the exact opposite
fixed-core movement of order q², both complete fixed attachments of order
q, and the full displacement of order q² containing the first attachment.
Both the attachment and displacement are disjoint from the opposite fixed
core. The point-radical quadratic-refinement calculation now sharpens the
fixed-core actor image to exactly q and gives [T : T₀D] = q. The critical
centralizer modulo D′ has order at most q³, yielding [D : D ∩ D′] ≤ q⁴.
For T₁ = T₀D′, the full attachment then gives [D : D ∩ T₁] ≤ q³ and
[T : T₁] ≤ q⁴. Fixed-vector lifting in the same full cover gives the reverse
inequality and forces the displacement into T₁ ∩ D. Its order q² and
its disjointness from D′ would give [D : D ∩ D′] ≥ q⁵, a contradiction.
Thus large movement is excluded and [Z′,Q] ≤ Z_t holds without an extra
movement premise, proving the midpoint containment in source (3.7)(2).
In source (3), a noncontained core now forces the full radical to be a plane
radical on the same natural cover, with both midpoint indices q squared and
full radical order q cubed. This excludes the full Sylow branch and proves
its core decomposition and both overlap indices without extra premises.
The whole-core dual cover now gives [Q,Z′] ≤ R D with R = [Z,Z′]. In the
noncontained-core branch, equal natural displacement planes and the full
opposite attachment give [T,Z′] = [Q,Z′], and the component intersection
gives [T₀,Z′] = R. The full root-fixer comparison now gives T₀ ≤ Z Q′.
The opposite overlap centralizes T₀ and factors through U D, so it lies in
Z_t D; its full-radical displacement lies in D. The full orthogonal-cover
calculation now shows that the plane radical generates all its movement
on the root-fixed hyperplane. Hence R would lie in D, contradicting its
order relative to the full fixed attachment. This excludes the noncontained
core and completes (3): an actual order-q critical image gives Q = U D,
X = Z and both overlap indices q. Paired fixed-kernel comparison and
source (5)/(6) selection still precede the field restriction.
For an actual order-q critical image, individual root values now compare
both complete fixed attachments without a size premise on the full radical.
They agree unless both have order q; a nontrivial common element also forces
equality. In the remaining unequal case they are disjoint complements in
the common critical displacement. Excluding that maximal case and treating
the order-q-squared critical image remain in source (4).
The common overlap is now the full centralizer of either critical layer
inside the other. The literal centralizer of this overlap modulo the common
displacement moves it disjointly from both complete fixed attachments.
The maximal unequal case is now excluded at critical index q. The opposite
attachment would equal the critical-root normalizer overlap. Intersecting
that actor with the actual radical gives trivial hyperplane movement and
bounds the radical image by q squared, contrary to saturation. Thus both
full attachments agree for every order-q critical image, including the
full-Sylow branch with its critical-order premise discharged. The order-q-
squared branch of (4), source (5)/(6), and the field restriction remain.
In the large critical-image branch, source (2) now forces the entire
saturated radical to fill a plane radical in the original cover. Both
midpoint indices are q squared. The refinement obstruction places each
complete attachment in the critical commutator, and natural displacement
equality lifts to R = [Z,T] = [Z′,T]. Both original endpoint markings and
core kernels are retained for the remaining hyperplane comparison.
The large branch now has a full Z-hyperplane of index q containing the
midpoint and common displacement. Its literal section centralizer moves
it in a field-sized complement to the complete attachment, and that product
is the full root displacement. The cover calculation retains an arbitrary
specified plane marking. The comparison with the opposite attachment and
source (5)/(6) still precede the field restriction.
The midpoint is now the exact kernel of both large critical actions. If
an opposite centralizer escapes the endpoint core, it has field index and
fills the hyperplane fixer modulo that core. Its full hyperplane movement
contains the entire opposite attachment and has index q over it. These
supply the first two claims of the escaping-centralizer argument on p.875;
the remaining attachment comparison and source (5)/(6) stay open.
The escaping large-image comparison now excludes containment of the
opposite movement in the first hyperplane movement when the full attachments
differ. Its natural image has field-power order, so smaller attachments
agree by the individual-commutator calculation. Thus an escaping unequal
pair has both complete attachments of order q. The full-size comparison
and the remaining source (4)--(6) steps still precede the field restriction.

For full attachments, the escaping movement intersection has order q.
The opposite field structure now gives the source field-span obstruction:
every nontrivially moving element of the opposite hyperplane centralizer
has an individual commutator outside the root movement. Selecting the root
element whose displacement is that intersection remains necessary to close
the full-size escape branch; the nonescaping and terminal branches remain.

The nonescaping section centralizer now has both field indices q.
It supplies the same full hyperplane complement, and every complement
element is an individual commutator of the original Z-layers. The comparison across all marked hyperplanes is now complete: exclusion of
rank-one opposite subactors makes every nonzero-direction evaluation
bijective. The full-cover correction formula constructs every individual
commutator outside an attachment. Hence the two complete fixed attachments
agree for every critical pair, completing source (4). Source (5)/(6), the
field restriction and block conclusion remain.

The Q₂ element-centralizer count in (5) is now proved on the actual full
plane cover. For x in Z outside the midpoint, the literal centralizer has
image of order q modulo the whole core, fixed indices q on U and Z, and
full-X displacement of index q over its intersection with the complete
attachment. The comparison of the two Z-layers using these counts remains,
followed by the critical-pair selection and final field/block assembly.

-/
